From 69684e03f66e7543255b9886e8134643443fc778 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ersin=20KO=C3=87?= <ersinkoc@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 20:07:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(node): a replayed revocation does not undo the owner's
 re-trust (ADR-0081)

A revocation dated ahead of the node's clock (accepted up to 5 minutes) and
received before the owner trusted the device again on the machine could be
dated after that re-trust, so the hub sending it again removed the device
again (audit F220).

The node now keeps each verified revocation dated ahead of its clock when it
arrives, by the fields its revoker signed, in <data>/device-revocations-ahead
(at most 256 lines, earliest-dated dropped first; a pending line goes once the
clock passes its date). `confirm` marks the device's lines dated at or after
its new `since` as overridden; a revocation with that identity then removes
nothing while the device keeps that `since`, and the log records
`overridden`. A revocation signed later, by another device, or not received
before the re-trust still applies. `confirm` no longer forgets the device's
line in device-revocations, so what it signed before its revocation stays
refused (T21). `agora node trust`, enroll and --from-hub print one line per
overridden revoker (new key cli-trust-revocation-overridden, en and tr).
